WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.421 --> 00:00:17.847
of the largest and most famous cities in the United States is San Francisco, but it hasn't always

2
00:00:17.927 --> 00:00:18.707
been popular.

3
00:00:19.628 --> 00:00:24.773
In fact, in 1848 there were only five hundred people living there.

4
00:00:25.694 --> 00:00:33.767
Only two years later in 1850—thanks to something called the Gold Rush—the city had grown to 25,000 people.

5
00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:42.452
On a cold morning in January 1848, James Marshall and John Sutter were on the banks of the

6
00:00:42.512 --> 00:00:44.573
American River building a sawmill.

7
00:00:45.393 --> 00:00:51.597
Marshall saw something glittering in the water, and when he pulled it out, he realized it was gold!

8
00:00:53.117 --> 00:00:58.921
Marshall and Sutter didn't want people to stop the construction of their sawmill, so they agreed to keep

9
00:00:58.961 --> 00:01:00.002
the gold a secret.

10
00:01:01.243 --> 00:01:07.548
Unfortunately for them Sam Brannan found out about the gold, and that is how the Gold Rush began.

11
00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.892
Sam Brannan was a brilliant businessman.

12
00:01:12.752 --> 00:01:18.237
Unlike Marshall and Sutter, he knew that he could make money from gold without even digging for it.

13
00:01:19.057 --> 00:01:24.121
First, Brannan bought every shovel, pan, and digging tool in San Francisco.

14
00:01:24.882 --> 00:01:29.133
Then, he ran through the streets yelling, "There's gold in the American River!

15
00:01:29.573 --> 00:01:30.694
Gold for everyone!

16
00:01:31.234 --> 00:01:31.975
We'll be rich!"

17
00:01:34.576 --> 00:01:36.717
Of course everyone wants to be rich.

18
00:01:37.638 --> 00:01:41.340
But to get rich with gold, people need tools to mine the gold.

19
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:50.585
In 1848 shovels usually cost 25 cents, but now that Brannan owned them all, he charged 15 dollars

20
00:01:50.685 --> 00:01:51.846
for each shovel!

21
00:01:52.566 --> 00:01:59.443
People were desperate for gold, and in only nine weeks, Brannan made $36,000 selling tools.

22
00:02:01.604 --> 00:02:06.769
Over the next year, stories about rivers full of gold in California spread east.

23
00:02:07.449 --> 00:02:12.954
People in the eastern United States were eager to mine gold, but there was no easy way to

24
00:02:12.994 --> 00:02:14.075
get to California.

25
00:02:14.895 --> 00:02:21.481
Some people crossed the plains in covered wagons, and other people traveled by boat around South America.

26
00:02:22.361 --> 00:02:27.309
Either way the trip took at least six months, and very few people survived.

27
00:02:28.809 --> 00:02:32.391
Many hopeful miners traveled to California using wagons.

28
00:02:33.231 --> 00:02:37.113
However, California is on the edge of the Nevada desert.

29
00:02:37.953 --> 00:02:42.795
Many people would run out of water and die in the desert when they were so close to

30
00:02:42.815 --> 00:02:43.876
the end of their journey.

31
00:02:46.197 --> 00:02:52.600
Californian businessmen decided to make money off this, so they rode into the desert with large barrels of

32
00:02:52.660 --> 00:03:00.060
water and sold glasses of water for one dollar, five dollars, and even one hundred dollars to thirsty

33
00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:06.683
travelers! The biggest years of the Gold Rush were 1848 through 1850.

34
00:03:07.904 --> 00:03:16.229
During those two years, people traveled to California from China, Mexico, Europe, and all over the United States

35
00:03:16.389 --> 00:03:17.269
to find gold.

36
00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:23.798
This made California an international cultural center unlike any other place in the world.

37
00:03:25.319 --> 00:03:32.764
Unfortunately of the 25,000 people who traveled to California to find gold, very few became rich.

38
00:03:33.685 --> 00:03:38.868
In fact, most of the people who became rich during the Gold Rush never lifted a shovel.

39
00:03:39.649 --> 00:03:43.832
The people who made the most money from the Gold Rush were those who took care of the

40
00:03:43.892 --> 00:03:51.261
miners. There were very few women in California at this time, so the ones who were there made

41
00:03:51.301 --> 00:03:53.502
a lot of money by taking care of the men.

42
00:03:54.803 --> 00:04:00.806
For example, one woman made $18,000 just by selling pies she baked in her oven.

43
00:04:02.687 --> 00:04:08.431
Another woman charged eight dollars just to wash a dozen pieces of laundry, when in other states it

44
00:04:08.491 --> 00:04:10.092
cost only 25 cents.

45
00:04:11.032 --> 00:04:18.296
Other women made dinner for twenty men every night and sold each plate of food for ten dollars!

46
00:04:18.336 --> 00:04:24.241
In any other state, these prices would have been robbery, but in California the miners were desperate and

47
00:04:24.301 --> 00:04:25.482
paid the high prices.

48
00:04:27.603 --> 00:04:32.867
Many men who left their homes to find gold in California also left their families behind.

49
00:04:32.887 --> 00:04:37.991
"I'll be home in a year, and we'll be richer than you can dream," a man would promise

50
00:04:38.031 --> 00:04:39.192
his family as he left.

51
00:04:40.292 --> 00:04:46.089
But with so many people in California, it became very difficult to find gold, and many men were

52
00:04:46.129 --> 00:04:48.130
not able to return to their families.

53
00:04:50.612 --> 00:04:53.975
Women were scarce in California, and the men were lonely.

54
00:04:54.856 --> 00:05:00.200
One man was able to find a bride in California, and when he got married, he charged men

55
00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:03.543
five dollars to come to the wedding to see his new wife.

56
00:05:04.323 --> 00:05:09.547
The men were so desperate to see a woman's face that they happily paid five dollars each!

57
00:05:10.828 --> 00:05:14.972
The story of the Gold Rush is a story of both riches and poverty.

58
00:05:15.692 --> 00:05:20.917
Though many people didn't become rich, in the end they created a very rich state.

59
00:05:21.718 --> 00:05:29.385
Today California celebrates its Gold Rush history in many ways— one of which is San Francisco's football team,

60
00:05:30.025 --> 00:05:35.290
named the 49ers after the many gold miners who arrived in 1849.

61
00:05:36.211 --> 00:05:41.356
Of course there may still be gold in that American River; no one really knows.
